      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-342425",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following differential equation.\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}{y}^{\u2033}+x(2x-3){y}^{\u2032}-3y=0\n$$\n\nThe general solution near $x=0$ of the differential equation is given by\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(x)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(x),\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are arbitrary constants and ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are linearly independent solutions. According to the Frobenius method, what is the best choice for the first solution ${y}_{1},$ where ${a}_{n}$ for $n=0,1,2,\u2026$ are constants determined by substitution?\n\n*Hint: You may assume $x=0$ is a regular singular point of the ODE and the indicial equation of the ODE is $2{\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda -3=0.$*"
